    int ret = crypto_init(name, key, sizeof(key), iv, sizeof(iv), &ctx);
    if (ret == -1) {
        printf("Error initializing cipher\n");
        return 1;
    }
    unsigned char in[] = "...";
    unsigned char out[1024] = {0};
    size_t len = sizeof(in);
    ret = crypto_update(name, ctx, in, len, out, &outlen);
    if (ret == -1) {
        printf("Error updating cipher\n");
        return 1;
    }
    ret = crypto_final(name, ctx, out + outlen, &outlen);
    if (ret == -1) {
        printf("Error finalizing cipher\n");
        return 1;
    }
    crypto_cleanup(name, ctx);
}
```

This is the basic skeleton of a cryptographic library that provides a set of functions for encrypting and decrypting data using symmetric key algorithms. The `crypto_init()`, `crypto_update()`, and `crypto_final()` functions are used to encrypt and decrypt data in chunks, while the `crypto_digest_init()`, `crypto_digest_update()`, and `crypto_digest_final()` functions are used for generating message digests.

The `crypto_sign()` and `crypto_verify()` functions are not yet implemented, but will be in a future version of the library. They provide support for digital signatures using asymmetric key algorithms like RSA or DSA.

This library is designed to be used by other programs that need access to cryptographic primitives like encryption and message digests. It provides a simple way to initialize, update, finalize, and clean up these operations.']

