  // Check if file exists and is not empty
  if (!req.file || !req.file.originalname || !req.file.size) {
    return res.status(400).json({ message: 'Invalid file' });
  }

  const filePath = path.join(__dirname, 'uploads', req.file.originalname);
  const fileSize = req.file.size;

  // Monitor upload progress and handle errors
  try {
    await upload(userId, fileName, filePath, fileSize);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    return res.status(500).json({ message: 'Internal server error' });
  } finally {
    // Delete local copy of the file
    await promisify(fs.unlink)(filePath);
  }
});
```

This is a more complex example that demonstrates the use of asynchronous programming, queuing, fallback strategies, monitoring upload progress, and handling errors in an Express app. The app has three endpoints: `/api/upload`, `/api/fallback`, and `/api/monitor`. The `/api/upload` endpoint allows users to upload files using a POST request with the `file` parameter. The `/api/fallback` endpoint falls back to the storage service if the local copy of the file is not available. The `/api/monitor` endpoint monitors the progress of the upload and handles errors. The app also has a queuing mechanism that allows multiple uploads to be processed concurrently, up to a maximum of 100 uploads at a time."]

