1. Design a resilient distributed semaphore system in C++ that dynamically manages concurrent access to shared resources across a cluster of nodes. The system must support creation, deletion, and dynamic adjustment of semaphores, informed by real-time application performance monitoring (APM) data and predictive models estimating future load. Ensure thread-safe and race-free access from multiple threads and nodes, integrating with a cluster management framework that handles node failures, dynamic load balancing, and seamless node addition/removal. Implement a self-healing mechanism that redistributes semaphore ownership and reassigns tasks upon node failures, minimizing downtime. Consider system dependencies and external data sources, and develop strategies to mitigate the impact of network latency and service outages on semaphore operations.
```c++
class Semaphore {
    private:
        int value; // current semaphore value
        std::mutex mutex; // mutex for synchronizing access to shared resources
        std::condition_variable cv; // condition variable for signaling semaphore availability
        std::unordered_set<std::thread> threads; // set of threads waiting on the semaphore
        std::unordered_map<int, std::shared_ptr<std::thread>> thread_id_to_thread; // map from thread ID to thread object
        int next_thread_id = 0; // next available thread ID
        bool is_destroyed = false; // flag indicating whether the semaphore has been destroyed
    public:
        Semaphore(int initial_value) : value(initial_value) {}
        ~Semaphore() {
            if (!is_destroyed) {
                std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
                cv.notify_all();
                is_destroyed = true;
            }
        }
        void destroy() {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
            if (!is_destroyed) {
                cv.notify_all();
                is_destroyed = true;
            }
        }
        int getValue() {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
            return value;
        }
        void post(int count) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
            cv.notify_all();
            value += count;
        }
        void wait() {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
            while (value == 0) {
                cv.wait(lock);
            }
            --value;
        }
};
```
