// Define class for managing a thread-safe memory allocator
class ThreadSafeMemoryAllocator {
public:
    // Constructor
    ThreadSafeMemoryAllocator() : allocators_(MAX_THREADS) {}

    // Allocate memory using the specified size and alignment
    void* allocate(size_t size, size_t alignment = std::alignment_of<std::max_align_t>::value) {
        // Get the current thread ID
        const auto threadId = std::this_thread::get_id();

        // Lock the allocator for the current thread
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(allocators_[threadId].mutex);

        // Check if there is a free slot available in the allocator
        auto& freeSlots = allocators_[threadId].freeSlots;
        for (auto it = freeSlots.begin(); it != freeSlots.end(); ++it) {
            void* memory = *it;
            if (alignment <= std::alignment_of<void>::value && (reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(memory) % alignment == 0)) {
                freeSlots.erase(it);
                return memory;
            }
        }

        // If no slot is available, allocate a new block of memory
        auto& blocks = allocators_[threadId].blocks;
        if (blocks.empty()) {
            blocks.push_back({});
            blocks.back().memory = std::aligned_alloc(alignment, size);
            blocks.back().size = size;
        } else if (blocks.back().size < size) {
            // If the last block is not large enough, allocate a new one
            auto& currentBlock = blocks.back();
            currentBlock.memory = std::aligned_alloc(alignment, size);
            currentBlock.size = size;
        } else if (blocks.back().memory == nullptr) {
            // If the last block is invalid, allocate a new one
            auto& currentBlock = blocks.back();
            currentBlock.memory = std::aligned_alloc(alignment, size);
            currentBlock.size = size;
        } else {
            // Try to use an existing block of memory that is large enough
            void* memory = nullptr;
            for (auto& block : blocks) {
                if (block.memory && block.size >= size) {
                    memory = block.memory;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (!memory) {
                // If no block is available, allocate a new one
                auto& currentBlock = blocks.back();
                currentBlock.memory = std::aligned_alloc(alignment, size);
                currentBlock.size = size;
            }
        }

        return blocks.back().memory;
    }

    // Deallocate memory using the specified pointer and alignment
    void deallocate(void* memory, size_t size, size_t alignment = std::alignment_of<std::max_align_t>::value) {
        // Get the current thread ID
        const auto threadId = std::this_thread::get_id();

        // Lock the allocator for the current thread
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(allocators_[threadId].mutex);

        // Check if the memory is a free slot in the allocator
        auto& freeSlots = allocators_[threadId].freeSlots;
        for (auto it = freeSlots.begin(); it != freeSlots.end(); ++it) {
            if (*it == memory) {
                return;
            }
        }

        // Add the memory to the list of free slots in the allocator
        freeSlots.push_back(memory);
    }

private:
    struct AllocatorBlock {
        void* memory = nullptr;
        size_t size = 0;
    };

    struct Allocator {
        std::mutex mutex;
        std::vector<void*> freeSlots;
        std::list<AllocatorBlock> blocks;
    };

    static constexpr int MAX_THREADS = 100;

    // Array of allocators for each thread, each allocator has a list of free slots and a list of allocated blocks
    Allocator allocators_[MAX_THREADS];
};
```

The `ThreadSafeMemoryAllocator` class is designed to be used as a drop-in replacement for the standard library's memory allocation functions, such as `new`, `delete`, and `malloc`. It provides thread-safe access to the underlying memory allocators, which allows multiple threads to allocate and deallocate memory without conflicts.

The `allocate` function first checks if there is a free slot available in the allocator's list of free slots that matches the specified size and alignment requirements. If a matching slot is found, it is removed from the list and returned as the allocated memory. If no slot is available, a new block of memory is allocated using `std::aligned_alloc` with the specified size and alignment requirements, and added to the allocator's list of blocks. The function then returns the address of the allocated memory.

The `deallocate` function first checks if the specified pointer is a free slot in the allocator's list of free slots. If it is, the function simply returns without doing anything. Otherwise, it adds the specified pointer to the list of free slots and returns.

The `MAX_THREADS` constant specifies the maximum number of threads that can use the allocator simultaneously. This value should be chosen based on the expected usage patterns of the application and the available system resources. A larger value may provide better performance, but may also consume more memory and increase contention between threads for access to the underlying memory allocators.

The `AllocatorBlock` struct represents a block of allocated memory that is managed by the allocator. It has two fields: `memory`, which points to the start of the allocated memory, and `size`, which specifies the size of the block in bytes. The `blocks` list stores all the allocated blocks in the allocator, and is used to track the available memory for allocation requests that do not fit within any existing block.

The `Allocator` struct represents an individual allocator instance that manages a set of free slots and allocated blocks for a specific thread. It has three fields: `mutex`, which is a mutex used to synchronize access to the underlying memory allocators, `freeSlots`, which is a list of free slots in the allocator, and `blocks`, which is a list of allocated blocks in the allocator.

The `ThreadSafeMemoryAllocator` class has two public member functions: `allocate` and `deallocate`. The `allocate` function is used to allocate memory with the specified size and alignment requirements, while the `deallocate` function is used to deallocate memory when it is no longer needed.

This implementation uses a thread-safe spinlock to synchronize access to the underlying memory allocators, which may reduce contention between threads for access to the allocator's list of free slots and allocated blocks. However, this approach can be expensive in terms of CPU usage, especially when there are many threads competing for access to the allocator's resources. An alternative approach is to use a lock-free data structure such as a hash table or a linked list to manage the free slots and allocated blocks, which may provide better performance at the cost of slightly higher memory usage."]

