class ExternalSystemUtility {

    // Define a constant for the maximum number of attempts
    private static final int MAX_ATTEMPTS = 3;

    // Define a method to make an external system call
    public static Object makeExternalCall(String url, String payload) throws IOException {
        // Set the initial delay and factor
        double delay = 1.0D;
        double factor = 2.0D;

        // Loop until the maximum number of attempts is reached or a successful response is received
        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < MAX_ATTEMPTS; attempt++) {
            try {
                // Make the external system call and return the response
                Object response = makeExternalCallInternal(url, payload);
                return response;
            } catch (IOException e) {
                // Log the error and continue attempting to connect
                System.out.println("Error making external call: " + e.getMessage());
            }

            // Calculate the delay for the next attempt
            double nextDelay = delay * factor;

            // Sleep for the calculated delay
            try {
                Thread.sleep((int) nextDelay);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                // Log the error and continue attempting to connect
                System.out.println("Error sleeping: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        // If all attempts fail, throw an exception
        throw new IOException("Maximum number of attempts reached");
    }

    // Define a method to make an external system call with retries
    private static Object makeExternalCallInternal(String url, String payload) throws IOException {
        URL requestUrl = new URL(url);
        HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) requestUrl.openConnection();
        connection.setRequestMethod("POST");
        connection.setDoOutput(true);
        connection.connect();
        OutputStreamWriter writer = new OutputStreamWriter(connection.getOutputStream());
        writer.write(payload);
        writer.flush();

        // Read the response from the external system
        InputStream inputStream = connection.getInputStream();
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(inputStream));
        StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            builder.append(line);
        }
        Object response = JSONObject.parse(builder.toString());

        // Close the connections
        writer.close();
        reader.close();
        inputStream.close();

        return response;
    }
}

In this implementation, we have defined a utility class called `ExternalSystemUtility` that provides methods for managing external system calls. The class has two main methods: `makeExternalCall` and `makeExternalCallInternal`.

The `makeExternalCall` method is the entry point for making an external system call. It takes two parameters: `url` and `payload`. It first attempts to make the call using `makeExternalCallInternal`, which returns the response from the external system. If the call fails, it will retry the call with an exponential backoff strategy.

The `makeExternalCallInternal` method makes the actual call to the external system and handles the response. It takes two parameters: `url` and `payload`. It creates a new `URL` object using the `url` parameter, opens a connection to the external system using the `openConnection` method of the `URL` class, sets the request method to "POST", enables output, connects to the external system, writes the payload to the output stream using an `OutputStreamWriter`, flushes the writer, reads the response from the input stream using a `BufferedReader`, parses the response as JSON using the `JSONObject.parse` method, and returns the parsed response.

We have also defined a constant called `MAX_ATTEMPTS` to control the maximum number of attempts to make an external system call. In this case, we are setting it to 3. If all attempts fail, the method will throw an exception.

In summary, using a utility class like this can help us manage external system calls more efficiently and provide better error handling and retry mechanisms for when the external systems are unavailable or experiencing issues.']

