// Version Control and Migration Strategy
public class VersionControl {
  // Version number of the current release
  private static final String CURRENT_VERSION = "1.0";

  // List of available versions for migration
  private static final List<String> AVAILABLE_VERSIONS = Arrays.asList("1.0", "2.0");

  // Migration strategy for updating to a new version
  public void migrateToVersion(String targetVersion) {
    if (!AVAILABLE_VERSIONS.contains(targetVersion)) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid version: " + targetVersion);
    }

    // Perform any necessary updates and migrations for the current version to the target version
    updateToVersion(CURRENT_VERSION, targetVersion);
  }

  private void updateToVersion(String fromVersion, String toVersion) {
    // Update code for the current version to the target version
    if (fromVersion.equals("1.0")) {
      // Upgrade from version 1.0 to version 2.0
      // ...
    } else if (toVersion.equals("2.0")) {
      // Upgrade from any earlier version to version 2.0
      // ...
    }

    // Update the current version number to the target version
    CURRENT_VERSION = toVersion;
  }
}
```
This architecture uses a version control system to manage updates and ensures backward compatibility while minimizing disruption to existing services. The `migrateToVersion` method performs any necessary updates and migrations for the current version to the target version, while the `updateToVersion` method updates the code for the current version to the target version.

For system dependencies and external data sources, the architecture ensures that the software component relies on stable and reliable third-party services and external data feeds by implementing fallback mechanisms when they are unavailable. It also provides clear diagnostics and fallback strategies for persistent issues, such as service outages or API changes.

