public static class BinaryDeserializer
{
    public static T Deserialize<T>(byte[] data) where T : class
    {
        try
        {
            using (var stream = new MemoryStream(data))
            {
                var formatter = new BinaryFormatter();
                return formatter.Deserialize(stream) as T;
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            throw new DeserializationException("Failed to deserialize data", ex);
        }
    }
}
```
This method uses the `BinaryFormatter` class to deserialize a byte array into an object of type `T`. The method first creates a `MemoryStream` from the input `data`, then passes it to the `Deserialize` method of the `BinaryFormatter`. If the deserialization fails, the method throws a `DeserializationException` with a meaningful message.

The method also handles cases where the class of the object being deserialized is not found, throwing a `ClassNotFoundException`.

To ensure codebase maintainability and testing, the method uses generics to handle different types of objects that can be deserialized. The `where T : class` constraint ensures that only reference types can be passed as input, making it easier to write unit tests for the method.

The method also includes a `try-catch` block to catch any exceptions that may occur during deserialization and throw a custom exception with a meaningful message. This allows developers to handle errors in a consistent way and provides better error handling than using the built-in `BinaryFormatter` methods directly.

