Here's a suggested design for the modular C++ library:

1. Custom Protocol Message Structure
---------------------------------------

To manage custom protocol messages, we will define a struct that encapsulates header information, payload data, and metadata tags for each message type. This structure can be extended or modified as needed to accommodate different message formats and requirements.
```c++
struct CustomProtocolMessage {
    std::string header; // Header information (e.g., message ID)
    std::vector<uint8_t> payload; // Payload data
    std::map<std::string, std::string> metadata; // Metadata tags (e.g., timestamp, sender)
};
```
2. Message Type and Encoding/Decoding Protocols
-----------------------------------------------

To support multiple message types and flexible encoding/decoding protocols, we can define a list of supported message types and their corresponding encoders and decoders. Each message type can have its own unique format and requirements for encoding and decoding.
```c++
struct MessageType {
    std::string name; // Name of the message type (e.g., "Text", "Binary")
    Encoder* encoder; // Function pointer to the encoder function
    Decoder* decoder; // Function pointer to the decoder function
};
```
3. Message Collection and Management
------------------------------------

To manage a dynamic collection of custom protocol messages, we can use a thread-safe data structure like `std::vector` or `std::list`. We will also implement fine-grained locks where necessary to ensure concurrent read and write access by multiple threads.
```c++
class MessageCollection {
public:
    void add(const CustomProtocolMessage& message); // Add a new message to the collection
    bool remove(const std::string& id); // Remove a message from the collection
    CustomProtocolMessage get(const std::string& id) const; // Get a message by its ID
    size_t size() const; // Return the number of messages in the collection
private:
    std::vector<CustomProtocolMessage> _messages; // Thread-safe data structure for storing messages
};
```
4. Message Acknowledgment and Retransmission Mechanism
---------------------------------------------------

To ensure data integrity and reliability, we can implement a message acknowledgment protocol that requires receivers to acknowledge the reception of each message. If a message is not acknowledged within a certain time period, it will be retransmitted until an acknowledgement is received. We will also maintain a record of all sent messages and their associated acknowledgements for easy recovery in case of failures.
```c++
class MessageSender {
public:
    void send(const CustomProtocolMessage& message); // Send a message to the network
    bool receive(const std::string& id, const CustomProtocolMessage& message); // Receive and process a message
private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, CustomProtocolMessage> _sentMessages; // Record of sent messages and their associated acknowledgements
};
```
5. System Integration and Performance Optimization
-----------------------------------------------

To integrate with existing network services and optimize performance, we can use a robust retransmission mechanism that takes into account variable delays, message loss, and retransmissions. We will also implement message acknowledgment protocols to ensure data integrity and reduce the impact of external service failures.
```c++
class NetworkService {
public:
    bool send(const CustomProtocolMessage& message); // Send a message to the network
    bool receive(const std::string& id, const CustomProtocolMessage& message); // Receive and process a message
private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, CustomProtocolMessage> _receivedMessages; // Record of received messages and their associated acknowledgements
};
```
6. Error Handling and Fallback Strategy
--------------------------------------

To handle malformed messages and encoding/decoding errors, we can implement a robust error handling mechanism that can recover from failures gracefully. We will also design a fallback strategy to mitigate the impact of external service failures and optimize recovery processes.
```c++
class ErrorHandler {
public:
    void handleError(const std::string& message); // Handle an error message
private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, CustomProtocolMessage> _errorMessages; // Record of error messages and their associated failures
};
```
7. Configuration Flexibility and Monitoring Tools
-----------------------------------------------

To allow for dynamic addition and removal of message types, we can use a configuration file or a database to store the list of supported message types and their corresponding encoders and decoders. We will also implement detailed logging and diagnostic tools to facilitate troubleshooting and performance analysis.
```c++
class Configuration {
