            return true;
        }

        private async Task<EventData> TransformAndEnrichAsync(EventData eventData)
        {
            try
            {
                // Implement data transformation and enrichment logic here
                return eventData;
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to transform and enrich message");
                throw;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `RabbitMQ` library to consume messages from a RabbitMQ queue, the `Kafka` library to consume messages from a Kafka topic, the `Azure.Messaging.EventHubs` library to consume messages from Azure Event Hubs, and the `Amazon.S3.Util` library to consume messages from an S3 bucket. We're also using the `Newtonsoft.Json` library to deserialize JSON data.

To handle message failures, we've added a retry mechanism to our `ProcessMessageAsync` method. If an exception is thrown while processing a message, it will be logged and retried after a delay. We've also implemented a schema validation logic using the `ValidateSchema` method to ensure that incoming data matches the expected schema.

To handle multiple messaging systems, we've abstracted the message consumption logic into separate methods for each system. This allows us to easily switch between different messaging systems without affecting our main processing logic. We've also implemented a data transformation and enrichment logic using the `TransformAndEnrichAsync` method. This allows us to preprocess the incoming messages before storing them in our database.

Overall, this is just one example of how you can use the `RabbitMQ`, `Kafka`, and `Azure.Messaging.EventHubs` libraries with the `Amazon.S3.Util` library to consume messages from multiple messaging systems and handle failures gracefully. You can adjust this code to suit your specific needs and requirements.']

