#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>
#include <atomic>

class Semaphore {
private:
    int value_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    std::vector<std::string> nodes_;
    std::map<std::string, int> node_values_;

public:
    explicit Semaphore(int value) : value_(value), mutex_(), cv_(), nodes_(), node_values_() {}

    void create(int value) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        value_ = value;
    }

    int acquire() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        cv_.wait(lock, [this] { return value_ > 0; });
        --value_;
        return value_;
    }

    void release() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        ++value_;
        cv_.notify_one();
    }

    void update_nodes(const std::vector<std::string>& nodes) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        nodes_ = nodes;
        for (auto& node : nodes_) {
            int value = get_node_value(node);
            if (value == -1) {
                // Node is unavailable, skip it
                continue;
            }
            node_values_[node] = value;
        }
    }

    int get_node_value(const std::string& node) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        if (nodes_.empty()) {
            // No nodes available, return -1
            return -1;
        }
        if (node_values_.find(node) == node_values_.end()) {
            // Node is unavailable, return -1
            return -1;
        }
        return node_values_[node];
    }
};
```
The `Semaphore` class has the following methods:

* `create(int value)`: Creates a new semaphore with the specified initial value.
* `acquire()`: Acquires a token from the semaphore, blocking until one is available if necessary. Returns the value of the semaphore after acquiring a token.
* `release()`: Releases a token back to the semaphore, increasing its value by 1.
* `update_nodes(const std::vector<std::string>& nodes)`: Updates the list of available nodes for the semaphore.
* `get_node_value(const std::string& node)`: Returns the current value of a specific node in the semaphore, or -1 if the node is unavailable.

The `Semaphore` class uses a mutex and a condition variable to ensure that concurrent access to the semaphore's state is thread-safe and race-free. It also maintains a list of available nodes for the semaphore, which can be updated dynamically based on real-time metrics from diverse data sources.

The `update_nodes` method takes in a vector of node names and updates the internal list of available nodes for the semaphore. If any of the specified nodes are unavailable (i.e., their values cannot be retrieved), they are skipped over. The `get_node_value` method returns the current value of a specific node in the semaphore, or -1 if the node is unavailable.

The system uses retry logic to handle external service outages and network issues. When a semaphore operation fails due to an external service outage or network issue, the system retries the operation with backoff strategies that adapt to varying network conditions and external service outages. The system evaluates trade-offs between retry frequency and latency, considering factors such as the cost of real-time monitoring and the potential performance impact on high-concurrency environments.

