```
To handle network failures gracefully, we will use a retry mechanism with exponential backoff. When a log message cannot be sent to the remote destination due to a network failure, the framework will retry sending it at an increasingly longer interval until it is successful or the maximum number of retries is reached.

To optimize memory and CPU usage, we will use a buffered approach where log messages are stored in a circular buffer before being sent to the remote destination. This will allow for efficient handling of high-throughput log volumes while minimizing performance impact during rotations.

To manage log rotation based on size thresholds or time-based intervals, we will use the boost::log library's built-in support for log rotation. This can be configured using a configuration file or other external means.

To encrypt sensitive information in logs destined for remote destinations, we will use the OpenSSL library to perform encryption and decryption of log messages. This will ensure compliance with security standards and prevent unauthorized access to sensitive data.

