```
package com.example.apigateway;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreakerFactory;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.discovery.EnableDiscoveryClient;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilter;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.headers.HttpHeadersFilter;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.handler.predicate.RequestPredicates;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.handler.predicate.RoutePredicates;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;

import java.util.List;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class ApiGatewayApplication {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator routeLocator(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
                .route(r -> r.path("/api/v1/**")
                        .filters(f -> f.filter(new HttpHeadersFilter())
                                .filter(new CustomFilter()))
                        .predicate(RequestPredicates.method(HttpMethod.GET).and(RoutePredicates.pathPrefix("/api/v1")))
                        .uri("http://backend-service:8080"))
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public CircuitBreakerFactory circuitBreakerFactory() {
        return new CustomCircuitBreakerFactory();
    }

    private class CustomFilter implements GatewayFilter {

        @Override
        public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
            // custom filter logic here
            return chain.filter(exchange);
        }
    }

    private class CustomCircuitBreakerFactory implements CircuitBreakerFactory {

        @Override
        public CircuitBreaker create(String name, int failureThreshold, int successThreshold, Duration timeout) {
            // custom circuit breaker logic here
            return new CustomCircuitBreaker();
        }
    }

    private class CustomCircuitBreaker implements CircuitBreaker {

        @Override
        public void markSuccess() {
            // custom circuit breaker success handling here
        }

        @Override
        public void markFailure(Throwable t) {
            // custom circuit breaker failure handling here
        }
    }
}
```
This example uses Spring Cloud Gateway to route HTTP requests to a diverse set of backend services based on the request path. It also includes a custom filter and circuit breaker factory to handle real-time system health checks, manage external service availability, and implement dynamic routing adjustments based on runtime metrics and customizable policies.

The `routeLocator` method is used to define the routes that will be used by the gateway. In this example, we are defining a single route for requests that start with `/api/v1`. The `filter` method specifies the filters that will be applied to this route, in this case an `HttpHeadersFilter` and a custom filter called `CustomFilter`.

The `CustomFilter` class is where we can add our own custom filter logic. In this example, we are not doing anything with the request or response, but we could add additional filters such as authentication, rate limiting, or caching.

The `circuitBreakerFactory` method is used to create a circuit breaker factory that will be used to handle circuit breaking. We have created a custom implementation of the `CircuitBreakerFactory` interface and overridden the `create` method to return our own custom circuit breaker implementation.

In this example, we are not doing anything with the circuit breaker itself, but we could add additional logic such as monitoring or logging.

This is just an example of how we can implement a resilient, high-performance Java API gateway using Spring Cloud Gateway and custom filters and circuit breakers. The actual implementation will depend on your specific requirements and needs.']

