In order to process packets using libpcap, we need to first initialize a handle for capturing packets. We can do this by calling the `pcap_open_live()` function, passing in the name of the interface that we want to capture from, along with the buffer size and promiscuous mode setting.

Once the handle is initialized, we can start capturing packets using the `pcap_loop()` function, which takes in a pointer to the handle and a callback function to be called for each packet that is received. We can also use the `pcap_next()` function to receive the next packet in a loop.

In our case, we want to capture all packets from a particular interface, so we pass in the name of the interface as the first argument to `pcap_open_live()`. We then set the buffer size and promiscuous mode accordingly, and initialize the handle using `pcap_open_live()`.

Once the handle is initialized, we can start capturing packets using `pcap_loop()` or `pcap_next()`. We can also use `pcap_breakloop()` to stop capturing packets at any point in time.

### Packet Processing with Scapy

To process packets using Scapy, we need to first import the necessary libraries. We can do this by importing the `scapy` library and calling the `init_all()` function.

Once the necessary libraries are imported, we can start capturing packets using the `sniff()` function, which takes in a filter expression that specifies the type of packets to capture. We can also use the `sinkhole()` function to capture all packets from a particular interface and process them using our functions defined earlier.

In our case, we want to capture all packets from a particular interface, so we pass in the name of the interface as the first argument to `sniff()`. We then define our functions for processing each type of packet and set up the filter accordingly.

Once the filters are defined, we can start capturing packets using `sniff()` or `sinkhole()`. We can also use `sendpkt` to send a packet out on an interface.

### Comparison

We compared the performance of libpcap and Scapy in capturing packets from a particular interface on our machine. The results are shown below:

| Library | Speed (Mpps) | Memory Usage (MB) | Packet Loss | Comments |
