Here is a sample implementation of the SyncMaster utility using Java:
```
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.io.IOException;
import org.apache.sshd.client.SshClient;
import org.apache.sshd.client.session.ClientSession;
import org.apache.sshd.common.config.keys.FilePasswordProvider;
import org.apache.sshd.common.keyprovider.KeyPairProvider;
import org.apache.sshd.common.util.closeable.AbstractCloseable;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class SyncMaster {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SyncMaster.class);

    private List<String> hosts;
    private Map<String, String> hostAliases;
    private KeyPairProvider keyProvider;
    private ExecutorService executor;
    private int threadCount;
    private int commandTimeout;
    private int retryCount;

    public SyncMaster(List<String> hosts, Map<String, String> hostAliases) {
        this.hosts = hosts;
        this.hostAliases = hostAliases;
        this.keyProvider = new FilePasswordProvider();
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(threadCount);
    }

    public void executeCommand(String command) {
        for (String host : hosts) {
            executor.execute(() -> {
                try (SshClient client = SshClient.setUpDefaultClient()) {
                    String alias = hostAliases.getOrDefault(host, host);
                    ClientSession session = client.startSession();
                    session.execCommand("ssh " + alias + " \"" + command + "\"");
                    session.waitForCondition(ClientSession::isOpen, commandTimeout);
                    if (session.isSuccess()) {
                        logger.info("Command executed successfully on host {}", alias);
                    } else {
                        logger.error("Error executing command on host {}", alias);
                    }
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    logger.warn("Error connecting to host {}", host, e);
                } finally {
                    session.close();
                }
            });
        }
    }

    public void syncFile(String localFilePath, String remoteFilePath) throws IOException {
        for (String host : hosts) {
            executor.execute(() -> {
                try (SshClient client = SshClient.setUpDefaultClient()) {
                    ClientSession session = client.startSession();
                    session.scpSend(localFilePath, remoteFilePath);
                    session.waitForCondition(ClientSession::isOpen, commandTimeout);
                    if (session.isSuccess()) {
                        logger.info("File {} synced successfully to host {}", localFilePath, host);
                    } else {
                        logger.error("Error syncing file {} to host {}", localFilePath, host);
                    }
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    logger.warn("Error connecting to host {}", host, e);
                } finally {
                    session.close();
                }
            });
        }
    }
}
```
The `SyncMaster` class has a list of hosts and a map of host aliases, which can be used to connect to multiple remote servers via SSH. It also has a key provider for public key authentication and an executor service for executing commands concurrently. The `executeCommand` method takes a command string as input and executes it on all the hosts in parallel using the `ClientSession#execCommand` method. It waits for the command to complete with a timeout of `commandTimeout` milliseconds, and logs any errors or success messages.

The `syncFile` method takes a local file path and a remote file path as input, and uses the `SshClient#scpSend` method to send the file to all the hosts in parallel. It also waits for the command to complete with a timeout of `commandTimeout` milliseconds, and logs any errors or success messages.

To implement the random number generator using the Mersenne Twister algorithm, we can create a new class called `MersenneTwisterRandom` that extends `AbstractCloseable`. This class should have a constructor for initializing the random number generator with a seed value, and a `nextInt` method that returns the next random integer.
```
public class MersenneTwisterRandom extends AbstractCloseable {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MersenneTwisterRandom.class);
    private Random random;

    public MersenneTwisterRandom() {
        this.random = new MersenneTwister();
    }

    public int nextInt() {
        return random.nextInt();
    }

    @Override
    public void close() throws IOException {
        logger.debug("Closing Mersenne Twister Random");
        this.random.close();
    }
}
```
To integrate the `MersenneTwisterRandom` class with the file synchronization and directory management functionality, we can modify the `SyncMaster` class to use an instance of `MersenneTwisterRandom` for generating random numbers instead of using `java.util.Random`. This will allow us to implement a more secure and reliable random number generator that is specifically designed for this application.
